[White "Benko - Fully accepted"]
[Event "Benko - Fully accepted"]
[Result "*"]
[ChapterName "Benko - Fully accepted"]
[Orientation "white"]
[StudyName "Reti - Reversed Benko"]

1. Nf3 d5 2. c4 d4 3. g3 c5 4. b4 cxb4 5. a3 bxa3 6. Bg2 Nc6 (6... e6!? { This will likely transpose into the mainline - except that the pawn will be on e6 instead of e5, which seems better for us. } 7. O-O Nc6 (7... Nf6 8. d3 { Not necessary, but increases the odds of transposing into the main line where black plays Nc6. } 8... Be7 { Nc6 is most common but transposes to the main line (e6 version). } 9. Bxa3 Bxa3 { Note that Nc6 would transpose into the mainline. } (9... O-O 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. Nxd4 e5 12. Nc2 Nc6 13. Nc3 { With plans of Nd5 and maybe Ne3. } { [%cal Gc3d5,Bc2e3] }) 10. Qa4+ Nc6 11. Qxa3) 8. d3 Nf6 9. Qa4 Bd7 10. Bxa3 Bxa3 11. Qxa3 Qe7 12. Nfd2 Qxa3 13. Nxa3 O-O 14. Rfb1 Rab8 15. Nb5 { [%cal Ga7a6,Gb5d6] }) (6... a2!? { [Rare 3%] } 7. Qa4+ Nc6 8. Ne5 Bd7 9. Nxd7 Qxd7 10. Rxa2 e5 11. O-O Nf6 12. d3 Be7 13. Ba3 O-O 14. Bxe7 Qxe7 15. Nd2 Rfc8 16. Qa3 { The queen trade is good for us.
The position is still close to equal, but our play should be easier.
Bring the other rook to a1 or b1.
f4 should probably be played eventually. } { [%cal Gf1a1,Bf1b1,Bf2f4] }) (6... Nf6 { [Rare 3%] } 7. d3 { [%cal Ge7e5] } 7... e5 { A sneaky move, not very common (6%).
The most common move was Nc6, but it likely transposes into the mainline after O-O, e5, Qa4. } 8. O-O Bd6 { Once again, Nc6 would transpose into the mainline. } 9. Nxa3 O-O 10. Nb5 Nc6 11. Nxd6 Qxd6 12. Ba3 Nb4 13. Qb3 a5 14. Bxb4) (6... g6!? { [Rare 4%]
Black plays g6 earlier than normal, we could try to punish it with Nxa3 and Qa4 - but it's more practical to let it transpose into the regular g6 line after Nc6, Nf6 & O-O } 7. O-O Bg7 8. d3 Nc6 9. Nxa3 Nf6 10. Bf4 Nh5 11. Bd2 { Same escape as with black in this situation } 11... O-O 12. Qa4 { In the original benko, we would've gone for Nb5 - but it's not as good here when e5 hasn't been played yet. 
Qa4 and Qb3 are almost always good moves in these positions. } { [%cal Ra3b5,Re7e5] } 12... Bd7 13. Rfb1 { Black can try chase our queen but it doesn't accomplish much: (13... Ne5 14. Qa5 b6 15. Qa6 Bc8 16. Qb5 Bd7) } { [%cal Gc6b8,Ga4b3,Bc6e5,Ba4a5,Bb7b6,Ba5a6] } 13... Rb8 14. Nc2 a6 15. Nb4 Nxb4 16. Qxb4 Bc6 17. Qb6 { A queen trade would be good for us. 
We'd continue with Bb4 and Nd2->Ne4. 
For example: (17... Qxb6 18. Rxb6 e5 19. Bb4 Rfe8 20. Nd2 Bxg2 21. Kxg2 Bf8 22. Bxf8 Kxf8 23. Ne4 Re6 24. Raxa6) } { [%cal Gd2b4,Bf3d2,Bd2e4] }) 7. O-O e5 (7... g6 8. d3 Bg7 9. Nxa3 { No need for Qa4 in this situation - with the bishop already on g7 and the knight on c6. } 9... Nf6 10. Bf4 { We get to play the same setup as the black benko against g3/g6. } 10... O-O 11. Ne5! { A new move that's normally not available in the black version. } { [%cal Gf3e5,Ra3b5] } 11... Nxe5 (11... Bd7 12. Qb3 Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Bc6 14. Nb5) 12. Bxe5 Nd7 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. Qb1 { Qb3 would be the normal move, but it walks into Nc5 with tempo. } { [%cal Rd1b3,Rd7c5] } 14... Nc5 15. Nb5 a6 16. Qb4 { Next we bring the other rook. } { [%cal Gf1b1] }) 8. d3 Nf6 (8... Bd6 9. Qa4 Bd7 10. Bxa3 Bxa3 11. Nxa3 { We capture with the knight because black can block the queen's diagonal with Ne7. } { [%cal Ra4a3,Rg8e7] } 11... Nge7 12. Rfb1 b6 13. Nb5 O-O 14. Nxe5 Nxe5 15. Bxa8 Qxa8 16. Qxa7 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gb5d4] }) (8... a2 { [Rare 2%]
Black's best move, only played 2% of the time. } 9. Nbd2 { If this square is available, we jump there with the knight. Now we can go after the pawn with the queen.

If the pawn is still on d2, we capture with the rook and go for e3. } { [%cal Gb1d2] } 9... Nf6 10. Qa4 Bd7 11. Qxa2 Bd6 12. Ba3 Qc7 13. Rfb1 O-O 14. Rxb7 { We can also take the bishop first, but this is better. We get a bishop and 2 pawns for the rook, and black will have two isolated pawns. 
Our bishop will be a beast on d6. } { [%csl Ra7,Rd4][%cal Ga3d6,Bf3e5] } 14... Qxb7 15. Bxd6 Rfe8 16. Nxe5 Qc8 17. Ndf3 a5 18. c5 { Opening up a mate threat while also protecting the bishop. } { [%csl Gf7][%cal Ga2f7] }) (8... f6) 9. Qa4 Bd7 (9... Bd6? 10. Nxd4 exd4 11. Bxc6+ Bd7 12. Bxd7+ Qxd7 13. Qxd7+ Kxd7 14. Nxa3 { Next we'll go after the center pawn with Nc2: (15. Nc2 Rxe2 16. Nxd4 Ree8 17. Nf5) }) (9... Nd7!? { [Rare]
Not as bad as it looks - still unpins the knight. } 10. Bxa3 Bxa3 11. Qxa3 Qe7 12. Nfd2!? { In the original benko, we'd go for e6 here. But with d3 already played, we have the option of putting a knight on d2. 

Both knight moves are equal - we go with the f-knight because it gives the biggest advantage when white plays Qxa3 (most common move). Then we'll have both knights ready on the queenside. } { [%cal Re2e3,Gf3d2] } 12... Qxa3 { The most likely move, however black doesn't need to trade.
Other continuations: (12... Nc5 13. Ne4 Nxe4 14. Bxe4)(12... a5 13. Qxe7+ Kxe7 14. Na3 a4 15. Rfb1 Nc5 16. Rb5 Kd6 17. Ne4+) } 13. Nxa3 O-O 14. Rfb1 Nc5 15. Nb5 { Next we can go Nb3 or Ne4. } { [%cal Ga7a6,Gd2e4,Bc8d7,Bd2b3] }) (9... Qc7!? { [Rare 1%] } 10. Nxe5 Qxe5 11. Bxc6+ Bd7 12. Bxd7+ Nxd7 13. Nd2 { Taking on e2 is too greedy: (13... Qxe2 14. Ne4 Qxd3 15. Re1 Be7 16. Nd6+) } { [%cal Ge5e2,Gd2e4,Be2d3,Bf1e1] }) (9... Be7 { [Rare 3%] } 10. Nxe5 Bd7 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Bxc6 O-O 13. Bxa3) 10. Bxa3 Bxa3 (10... Be7 11. Qb5 { CONTINUE }) 11. Qxa3 Qe7 12. Nfd2 Qxa3 13. Nxa3 O-O (13... Ke7 14. Nb3 b6 (14... a6 15. Nc5) 15. c5 Rhb8 16. Nc4 { [%cal Gb6b5,Gg2c6,Bc4e5] }) 14. Rfb1 Rab8 (14... b6 15. Nb5 Rfc8 16. Ra6 { [%cal Gb1a1] }) (14... Rfb8 15. Nb3 a5 16. Nc5 { [%cal Ga3b5,Bc6b4,Bg2b7] }) 15. Nb5 a6 (15... a5 16. c5 { [%cal Gb5d6] } 16... b6 17. Bxc6 Bxc6 18. Na7) 16. Nd6 b5 17. Rxa6 bxc4 18. Bxc6 Bxc6 19. Rxc6 c3 20. Rxb8 Rxb8 21. Nb3 h5 22. Rc8+ *
